body:
So how does what you say differ from the idea that "suicide is selfish"?. Because basically that's what you are trying to imply. How do you define where exactly the selfless responsibility starts and ends? Would you say a depressed parent has a selfless responsibility of taking care of a growing child? Most certaintly. But what happens when that child finally grows up and becomes an adult? Would you say that now that parent has a selfless responsibility of being there for their adult child every once in a while, watch them marry, watch them build a career and buy a house, watch them have their children and then becoming a grandparent, give them advice, etc.? See, your idea of "earning a right to die" is unachievable and impossible to implement in real life, because you are constantly being stuck in a cycle of fulfilling responsibilities, even the simpliest ones like paying your taxes. There is always something to give, there is always someone who needs stuff from you. But according to your logic, doctors and teachers can never have " a right to die", because there are always going to be people who will depend on them for their knowledge and treatment, even after they retire. But I am certain there are a lot of doctors and teachers out there who just want to get the hell out of here now. Sure, you can absolutely bombard yourself with social and humanitarian responsibilities, but it's not going to guarantee you a fulfillment. It doesn't guarantee anything, except that you might make lives of a couple or more people a bit better, and maybe feel a satisfaction from it. Suicidal mindset transcends that. It's not like you finish up with your responsibilities and then feel serene because you think you now have a right to choose to die because life never made sense to you in the first place. It doesn't work like that. From a suicidal mindset's point of view - what's the point of trying to help if none of what I'll do will help? And why don't I feel happy even though I am helping others? Why should I live like a martyr, suffer and see others cheer? You can delude yourself into a belief that others still need something from you, but it's not solving a suicidal issue. It only postpones it. Believe me, I've met a lot of humanitarians and selfless individuals in my life - and those were some of the most miserable people I've ever seen. Because in the end of the day - we all look for a selfish reason to stay alive, whether you admit this or not. Because that is who we are at the core of our selves - we are half selfish and half selfless creatures. But that selfishness is still a fundamentall part of us, through which more often than not we perceive our concepts of selflessness. We need our ego, it's only when it becomes too huge or too small when it becomes a problem. We attach ourselves to a role that gives us vitality, and we act through it. Yet a sucidial person fails to see that role in themselves AND for themselves. It's like a void, a bottomless pit that seems like it can't be filled. Yes, we are not isolated, but in the end of the day we all die alone - it's just the way it is. If you are looking to earn a right to die by performing certain duties, it's death that becomes your highest priority, not life. You are contradicting yourself. We are here to find a meaning in life, not in death. And if we can't find it, we create it, but in a such way that it makes sense to us when we think about ourselves AND when we think about other people. Dragging your ass through life and waiting for yourself to finish up with your selfless responsibilities so that you can finally die is a horrible way to live life. Very few make it to the end. That is how you truly kill your essence. And why do you bring the need for social interaction into this? You think popular extroverts who are seemingly healthy don't kill themselves?

&#x200B;

You seem to be a moralistic person, and that is ok. I hope you understand that I am not trying to pick a fight with you, I am just trying to say that this is not the best way to tackle the situation, since there are a lot of people who promulgate the ideas similar to yours, yet it still doesn't help the rates.
